.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/arg Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Arg"
.PP
\fRarg\fP is an unopinionated, no-frills CLI argument parser\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install arg
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
\fRarg()\fP takes either 1 or 2 arguments:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Command line specification object (see below)
.IP "2." 4
Parse options (\fIOptional\fP, defaults to \fR{permissive: false, argv: process\&.argv\&.slice(2), stopAtPositional: false}\fP)
.PP

.PP
It returns an object with any values present on the command-line (missing options are thus missing from the resulting object)\&. Arg performs no validation/requirement checking - we leave that up to the application\&.

.PP
All parameters that aren't consumed by options (commonly referred to as "extra" parameters) are added to \fRresult\&._\fP, which is \fIalways\fP an array (even if no extra parameters are passed, in which case an empty array is returned)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const arg = require('arg');

// `options` is an optional parameter
const args = arg(
    spec,
    (options = { permissive: false, argv: process\&.argv\&.slice(2) })
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ node \&./hello\&.js \-\-verbose \-vvv \-\-port=1234 \-n 'My name' foo bar \-\-tag qux \-\-tag=qix \-\- \-\-foobar
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// hello\&.js
const arg = require('arg');

const args = arg({
    // Types
    '\-\-help': Boolean,
    '\-\-version': Boolean,
    '\-\-verbose': arg\&.COUNT, // Counts the number of times \-\-verbose is passed
    '\-\-port': Number, // \-\-port <number> or \-\-port=<number>
    '\-\-name': String, // \-\-name <string> or \-\-name=<string>
    '\-\-tag': [String], // \-\-tag <string> or \-\-tag=<string>

    // Aliases
    '\-v': '\-\-verbose',
    '\-n': '\-\-name', // \-n <string>; result is stored in \-\-name
    '\-\-label': '\-\-name' // \-\-label <string> or \-\-label=<string>;
    //     result is stored in \-\-name
});

console\&.log(args);
/*
{
    _: ["foo", "bar", "\-\-foobar"],
    '\-\-port': 1234,
    '\-\-verbose': 4,
    '\-\-name': "My name",
    '\-\-tag': ["qux", "qix"]
}
*/
.fi
.PP

.PP
The values for each key=>value pair is either a type (function or [function]) or a string (indicating an alias)\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
In the case of a function, the string value of the argument's value is passed to it, and the return value is used as the ultimate value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
In the case of an array, the only element \fImust\fP be a type function\&. Array types indicate that the argument may be passed multiple times, and as such the resulting value in the returned object is an array with all of the values that were passed using the specified flag\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
In the case of a string, an alias is established\&. If a flag is passed that matches the \fIkey\fP, then the \fIvalue\fP is substituted in its place\&.
.PP

.PP
Type functions are passed three arguments:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
The parameter value (always a string)
.IP "2." 4
The parameter name (e\&.g\&. \fR--label\fP)
.IP "3." 4
The previous value for the destination (useful for reduce-like operations or for supporting \fR-v\fP multiple times, etc\&.)
.PP

.PP
This means the built-in \fRString\fP, \fRNumber\fP, and \fRBoolean\fP type constructors "just work" as type functions\&.

.PP
Note that \fRBoolean\fP and \fR[Boolean]\fP have special treatment - an option argument is \fInot\fP consumed or passed, but instead \fRtrue\fP is returned\&. These options are called "flags"\&.

.PP
For custom handlers that wish to behave as flags, you may pass the function through \fRarg\&.flag()\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const arg = require('arg');

const argv = [
    '\-\-foo',
    'bar',
    '\-ff',
    'baz',
    '\-\-foo',
    '\-\-foo',
    'qux',
    '\-fff',
    'qix'
];

function myHandler(value, argName, previousValue) {
    /* `value` is always `true` */
    return 'na ' + (previousValue || 'batman!');
}

const args = arg(
    {
        '\-\-foo': arg\&.flag(myHandler),
        '\-f': '\-\-foo'
    },
    {
        argv
    }
);

console\&.log(args);
/*
{
    _: ['bar', 'baz', 'qux', 'qix'],
    '\-\-foo': 'na na na na na na na na batman!'
}
*/
.fi
.PP

.PP
As well, \fRarg\fP supplies a helper argument handler called \fRarg\&.COUNT\fP, which equivalent to a \fR[Boolean]\fP argument's \fR\&.length\fP property - effectively counting the number of times the boolean flag, denoted by the key, is passed on the command line\&.\&. For example, this is how you could implement \fRssh\fP's multiple levels of verbosity (\fR-vvvv\fP being the most verbose)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const arg = require('arg');

const argv = ['\-AAAA', '\-BBBB'];

const args = arg(
    {
        '\-A': arg\&.COUNT,
        '\-B': [Boolean]
    },
    {
        argv
    }
);

console\&.log(args);
/*
{
    _: [],
    '\-A': 4,
    '\-B': [true, true, true, true]
}
*/
.fi
.PP
.SS "Options"
If a second parameter is specified and is an object, it specifies parsing options to modify the behavior of \fRarg()\fP\&.
.SS "\fRargv\fP"
If you have already sliced or generated a number of raw arguments to be parsed (as opposed to letting \fRarg\fP slice them from \fRprocess\&.argv\fP) you may specify them in the \fRargv\fP option\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const args = arg(
    {
        '\-\-foo': String
    },
    {
        argv: ['hello', '\-\-foo', 'world']
    }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
results in:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const args = {
    _: ['hello'],
    '\-\-foo': 'world'
};
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpermissive\fP"
When \fRpermissive\fP set to \fRtrue\fP, \fRarg\fP will push any unknown arguments onto the "extra" argument array (\fRresult\&._\fP) instead of throwing an error about an unknown flag\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const arg = require('arg');

const argv = [
    '\-\-foo',
    'hello',
    '\-\-qux',
    'qix',
    '\-\-bar',
    '12345',
    'hello again'
];

const args = arg(
    {
        '\-\-foo': String,
        '\-\-bar': Number
    },
    {
        argv,
        permissive: true
    }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
results in:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const args = {
    _: ['\-\-qux', 'qix', 'hello again'],
    '\-\-foo': 'hello',
    '\-\-bar': 12345
};
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRstopAtPositional\fP"
When \fRstopAtPositional\fP is set to \fRtrue\fP, \fRarg\fP will halt parsing at the first positional argument\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const arg = require('arg');

const argv = ['\-\-foo', 'hello', '\-\-bar'];

const args = arg(
    {
        '\-\-foo': Boolean,
        '\-\-bar': Boolean
    },
    {
        argv,
        stopAtPositional: true
    }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
results in:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const args = {
    _: ['hello', '\-\-bar'],
    '\-\-foo': true
};
.fi
.PP
.SS "Errors"
Some errors that \fRarg\fP throws provide a \fR\&.code\fP property in order to aid in recovering from user error, or to differentiate between user error and developer error (bug)\&.
.SS "ARG_UNKNOWN_OPTION"
If an unknown option (not defined in the spec object) is passed, an error with code \fRARG_UNKNOWN_OPTION\fP will be thrown:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// cli\&.js
try {
    require('arg')({ '\-\-hi': String });
} catch (err) {
    if (err\&.code === 'ARG_UNKNOWN_OPTION') {
        console\&.log(err\&.message);
    } else {
        throw err;
    }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
node cli\&.js \-\-extraneous true
Unknown or unexpected option: \-\-extraneous
.fi
.PP
.SH "FAQ"
.PP
A few questions and answers that have been asked before:
.SS "How do I require an argument with \fRarg\fP?"
Do the assertion yourself, such as:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const args = arg({ '\-\-name': String });

if (!args['\-\-name']) throw new Error('missing required argument: \-\-name');
.fi
.PP
